<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panama Canal Zone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> Zone was a strip of territory about ten miles wide along the Panama Canal, administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1903 to 1979 under the Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty. It had its own government, courts, police and schools, and its American residents, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zonians">Zonians</a>, lived largely apart from Panamanians. The riots of January 1964 over the right to fly the Panamanian flag hastened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a>, under which the zone was abolished on 1 October 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone.jpg" alt="Panama Canal Zone" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BMacZero · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Panama_Canal_Zone.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
